      "latency_ms": 2323.9,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 508,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a transparent, odorless, and tasteless chemical substance that is the main constituent of Earth's streams, lakes, and oceans, and the fluids of most living organisms. \n\nTo truly understand water, it helps to look at it through a few different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\nChemically, water is a molecule made up of three atoms: **two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom**. This is why its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**. \n\nWater has several unique and remarkable chemical properties:\n* **The Universal Solvent:** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it carries vital nutrients in our bodies and minerals through the soil.\n* **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some insects to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water move up the roots of plants).\n* **Density Anomaly:** Most substances shrink and become denser when they freeze, but water actually *expands* when it turns to ice. This is why ice floats, which prevents lakes and oceans from freezing solid from the bottom up, preserving aquatic life.\n\n### 2. The Physical View (The Three States)\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists in three distinct forms depending on the temperature:\n* **Liquid:** Water as we most commonly know it (rivers, rain, oceans).\n* **Solid:** Ice (glaciers, snow, ice cubes).\n* **Gas:** Water vapor or steam (humidity in the air, clouds, steam from a boiling kettle).\n\n### 3. The Ecological and Biological View\nWater is the foundation of life as we know it. \n* **For the Planet:** About 71% of the Earth's surface is covered by water, mostly in oceans (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). It drives our weather patterns, regulates the global climate, and shapes the landscape through erosion.\n* **For Living Things:** The human body is made up of about 60% water. Cells require water to function, chemical reactions in the body take place in water-based fluids, and it helps regulate body temperature through perspiration. Without water, humans can only survive for about 3 to 5 days.\n\n###"
